  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 127 AM EST Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unseasonably warm conditions are expected today, with \nmid 50s to lower 60s across the region. For today, the bar is rather \nlow for daily record high temperatures at Massena and Plattsburgh, \nand they appear likely to tie or break them today. A broad area of \nlow pressure will lift northwards along the US East Coast Sunday \nthrough Monday. Its disorganized nature has kept forecast guidance \nfrom coming to much agreement. Consensus appears to be trending back \ntowards a more eastward solution. The 00z EC and GFS went sharply \nfarther east with rain almost entirely missing the forecast area, \nexcept right along the Vermont-New Hampshire border. We do need the \nrain, and it feels bad that we're heading towards our lower end \nscenario again. The adage seems to be holding true, \"when in \ndrought, leave it out\". The gradient will be sharp, with about 0.33-\n0.75\" along the Upper Valley, around 0.10-0.33\" for the southern \nChamplain Valley up into the Northeast Kingdom, and tapering to a \nfew hundredths to the northwest. A few lingering showers could pass \neast with a sharp cold front with limited moisture. The warm will be \nreplaced with brisk northwest winds and temperatures sinking into \n20s Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: In the wake of the departing coastal low on Monday, \nnorthwest flow Monday will continue to be blustery as sinking colder \nCanadian air filters into the region. Temperatures will fall Monday \nnight some 20 to 25 degrees from our daytime highs on Monday, with \nTuesdays highs also 20 degrees cooler in the mid to upper 30s. \nNorthwest winds 10 to 15 mph, with gusts 20 to 25 mph will continue \nTuesday with strong caa, pushing widespread wind chills into the low \nto mid 20s through Tuesday night. Cold air will remain locked in \nplace through mid week as anomalously strong surface high pressure \ntracks over the region. Pressure fields for this surface high are \ninto the 99th percentile with new model guidance depicting around a \n1040mb high. Expect a slow moderate of the cold air as the high \nbuilds in through mid to late week, resulting in midday temperatures \nclimbing from the mid to upper 30s on Wednesday to the upper 30s to \nlow 40s by Friday. Moreover, such a strong high will drastically \ncutoff any precipitation chances Tuesday and Wednesday, keeping \nweather impacts for the pre-holiday travel low or non-existent.\n\nModels continue to depict a potential coastal system, or double-\nbarrel type system by Thanksgiving, but model spread remains too \nwide to nail any details. That said, thermal profiles would be \nsupportive of mainly rain in the Champlain Valley, with possibly a \nwintry mix into portions of the eastern Greens and Adirondacks based \non ensemble 850-925mb temperatures hovering about 0C. Furthermore, \ngiven the strength of the departing surface high, a tight pressure \ngradient over the area may be conducive for some corresponding gusty \nwinds with the potential system as well. While there is the \npotential for an impactful system late next week, there are still a \nnumber of ensembles members that keep us dry through next week, and \ngiven the preceding anomalous high, pattern recognition would \nsuggest models are too fast with the departing high, and move the \ncoastal system in too fast, and too far north and west. Furthermore, \nensemble MSLP for the coastal system appears to weak for what is \ncurrently being initialized in the models. That said, we will \ncontinue to monitor trends to this system closely, as it revolves \naround Thanksgiving.",
